WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.212 --> 00:00:02.148
- Who are we asking for permission

00:00:02.148 --> 00:00:03.221
to do what we want to do?

00:00:03.221 --> 00:00:04.804
That should be eradicated.

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:08.047
So if you are not paying
attention to your intention,

00:00:08.047 --> 00:00:11.592
then your life is just kind
of is a bit of a hot mess.

00:00:11.592 --> 00:00:13.512
You get in the elevator
after an interview,

00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:15.858
and they're just bitching
and moaning, or you're ...

00:00:15.858 --> 00:00:19.220
This bad thing that's happening
is not happening to you,

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.456
it's happening for you.

00:00:20.456 --> 00:00:22.319
If your dream only includes
you, it's too small.

00:00:22.319 --> 00:00:24.859
I've been crew, so I know
how it feels to be on a set

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:28.654
where there's not warmth
emanating from behind the camera.

00:00:28.654 --> 00:00:31.862
I look at each year that's
past, and all I've tried to do

00:00:31.862 --> 00:00:33.566
is continue to make work.

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:34.829
Favorite thing that say my mom is,

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:36.111
treat others like you'd
like to be treated.

00:00:36.111 --> 00:00:37.657
She always, always says that.

00:00:37.657 --> 00:00:40.182
The last film that I had in theaters,

00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:42.582
I distributed myself on 22 screens.

00:00:42.582 --> 00:00:44.188
Now it's on 2,300 screens.

00:00:44.188 --> 00:00:45.880
When you're a creative person,

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.548
and you're nurturing that within yourself,

00:00:47.548 --> 00:00:50.205
you open yourself up to a lot of discovery

00:00:50.205 --> 00:00:51.964
that comes from unexpected places,

00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:53.348
which is really wonderful.

00:00:53.348 --> 00:00:54.637
- She's an American director,

00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:57.549
screenwriter, film marketer
and film distributor.

00:00:57.549 --> 00:01:01.014
At the 2012 Sundance Film
Festival she won Best Director

00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:03.517
for her feature film Middle of Nowhere.

00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:06.157
She's the first black, female
director to be nominated for

00:01:06.157 --> 00:01:07.733
a Golden Globe Award.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.013
She's Ava DuVernay,

00:01:09.013 --> 00:01:11.597
and here are her top 10 rules for success.

00:01:11.597 --> 00:01:14.413
(jet engine)

00:01:14.413 --> 00:01:16.197
- You know we have to
work without permission,

00:01:16.197 --> 00:01:18.509
especially as women in this industry.

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:21.397
Who are we asking for permission
to do what we want to do?

00:01:21.397 --> 00:01:23.285
That should be eradicated.

00:01:23.285 --> 00:01:25.861
You need to set a path, and start walking.

00:01:25.861 --> 00:01:27.997
And that is what I've tried to do,

00:01:27.997 --> 00:01:29.669
and what I try to encourage folks to do.

00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:31.221
Walk with me, let me walk with you.

00:01:31.221 --> 00:01:32.541
Let's walk with each other.

00:01:32.541 --> 00:01:34.317
I think, you know, so much of the industry

00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:35.511
is very permission-based.

00:01:35.511 --> 00:01:36.751
And once you get out of your mind

00:01:36.751 --> 00:01:39.438
that you have to ask anyone
to do what you want to do,

00:01:39.438 --> 00:01:41.534
you'll be better for it.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:44.818
The intention of our attention.

00:01:44.818 --> 00:01:47.022
It's so big for everyone,

00:01:47.022 --> 00:01:51.840
but especially for artists
and creative-types like us.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.715
It's the cause that produces the effect,

00:01:54.715 --> 00:01:55.938
and the effect is our life.

00:01:55.938 --> 00:01:57.977
So whether you're conscious of it or not,

00:01:57.977 --> 00:01:59.706
your intention is the cause

00:01:59.706 --> 00:02:02.009
that produces the effect
which is your life.

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:05.449
So if you are not paying
attention to your intention,

00:02:05.449 --> 00:02:08.849
then your life is just kind
of is a bit of a hot mess.

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:10.121
That's what I've found.

00:02:10.121 --> 00:02:12.585
Each decision we make and action we take

00:02:12.585 --> 00:02:14.249
is born out of intention.

00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:16.169
So every thought, person, experience

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:20.143
that we give our attention
to, is motivated by intention.

00:02:20.143 --> 00:02:21.834
- [Interviewer] You've
been in this room before

00:02:21.834 --> 00:02:23.464
as an employee, I think, before you ...

00:02:23.464 --> 00:02:25.296
- Yeah, no, I did work for Paramount.

00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:26.129
A lot of work for Paramount

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:27.723
when I had my agency and my PR firm.

00:02:27.723 --> 00:02:29.715
- [Interviewer] So you
know what the business side

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:30.619
of all of this is.

00:02:30.619 --> 00:02:32.371
You know what the machinery
of all of this is.

00:02:32.371 --> 00:02:33.227
- Mmhmm, mmhmm.

00:02:33.227 --> 00:02:35.925
- [Interviewer] You really
do seem to be having

00:02:35.925 --> 00:02:40.113
a great time going through
this experience at this point.

00:02:40.113 --> 00:02:40.946
Is that fair?

00:02:40.946 --> 00:02:43.439
- Yeah, I think having done the
publicity for so many years,

00:02:43.439 --> 00:02:45.306
and been a part of that machine,

00:02:45.306 --> 00:02:48.849
I really would always watch
filmmakers and just wonder --

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:52.930
they were just so entrenched
in it in a way that was,

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.746
they were missing the joy of it.

00:02:54.746 --> 00:02:55.994
I would see it, you know, when you're

00:02:55.994 --> 00:02:58.522
standing off to the
side and you see someone

00:02:58.522 --> 00:03:01.538
just so bogged down in press,

00:03:01.538 --> 00:03:03.546
and in numbers,

00:03:03.546 --> 00:03:05.186
and in deals,

00:03:05.186 --> 00:03:06.570
that they were missing the fact

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.506
that they were living their dream.

00:03:08.506 --> 00:03:11.144
You know truly, they were ... you know,

00:03:11.144 --> 00:03:13.137
you get in the elevator with
someone after an interview,

00:03:13.137 --> 00:03:15.383
and they're just bitchin' and moanin'.

00:03:15.383 --> 00:03:18.377
They're trying on beautiful dresses,

00:03:18.377 --> 00:03:20.812
but they're upset because

00:03:20.812 --> 00:03:22.799
they didn't get the
dress that they wanted.

00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:23.983
Or, you know what I mean?

00:03:23.983 --> 00:03:25.949
They're sitting in an award show,

00:03:25.949 --> 00:03:27.749
but they're not sitting where
the other person was sitting.

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:29.257
I just always remember, it's like,

00:03:29.257 --> 00:03:31.674
"Dude, you've got to make a film."

00:03:31.674 --> 00:03:33.203
You know what I mean?

00:03:33.203 --> 00:03:34.744
Everyone's talking about it,

00:03:34.744 --> 00:03:37.327
or everyone's not, or whatever.

00:03:37.327 --> 00:03:38.896
But you made something.

00:03:38.896 --> 00:03:40.041
You did it.

00:03:40.041 --> 00:03:43.070
So I just never want to be that way.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:44.238
I really, really don't.

00:03:44.238 --> 00:03:45.286
And I just don't feel that way.

00:03:45.286 --> 00:03:47.642
I just literally ... I'm just happy

00:03:47.642 --> 00:03:49.352
that I get to make films.

00:03:49.352 --> 00:03:52.767
But one thing she said to
me that really sunk in deep,

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:55.017
and really changed my life.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.184
She said ... I was going
through a challenge,

00:03:59.184 --> 00:04:01.496
really tough challenge, and she said,

00:04:01.496 --> 00:04:06.464
"This is not happening to you,
this is happening for you.

00:04:06.464 --> 00:04:09.611
"This bad thing that's happening,
is not happening to you,

00:04:09.611 --> 00:04:11.867
"it's happening for you.

00:04:11.867 --> 00:04:14.555
"You're not the victim of it,
you're the beneficiary of it."

00:04:14.555 --> 00:04:17.819
And to think about anything that's going,

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:21.486
any struggle, any issue,
any personal crisis

00:04:22.573 --> 00:04:26.241
from the point of view of,
this is in my path for a reason

00:04:26.241 --> 00:04:28.741
changes the dynamic of the way

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.821
that you are in the situation

00:04:31.821 --> 00:04:34.724
and allows you to see there is going to be

00:04:34.724 --> 00:04:36.350
a light there somewhere.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:39.428
And also, it's not just
everything's going to work out.

00:04:39.428 --> 00:04:40.284
You know what I mean?

00:04:40.284 --> 00:04:43.811
It is, challenge yourself
to analyze this moment,

00:04:43.811 --> 00:04:46.951
and figure out what is really going on.

00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:49.775
That's, you know, requires a stamina,

00:04:49.775 --> 00:04:52.703
and a dignity, and a presence of mind

00:04:52.703 --> 00:04:54.984
that takes you out of
the woe is me moment,

00:04:54.984 --> 00:04:56.975
and empowers you.

00:04:56.975 --> 00:04:59.439
(piano music)

00:04:59.439 --> 00:05:01.827
Every day I say to
myself in some instance,

00:05:01.827 --> 00:05:03.276
at some level or another,

00:05:03.276 --> 00:05:05.556
"This isn't happening to
me, it's happening for me."

00:05:05.556 --> 00:05:07.260
And you know, I think back to times

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.244
when I was nursing my aunt,
in the last days of her life

00:05:10.244 --> 00:05:13.802
with breast cancer, and
just being a caregiver,

00:05:13.802 --> 00:05:17.669
and thinking at that time,
"Why is this happening to us?"

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:20.658
To be able to look back
at those hard times

00:05:20.658 --> 00:05:23.426
and reassess them from
the point of view of

00:05:23.426 --> 00:05:25.928
"This wasn't happening to
us, it was happening for us."

00:05:25.928 --> 00:05:28.687
I see the beautiful moments
we were able to share.

00:05:28.687 --> 00:05:30.550
The things we were able
to say to each other

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.670
in her last days that we
had never said before.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:37.046
The gifts that we were
able to give each other

00:05:37.046 --> 00:05:41.258
through bonding, through
connection, through reflection.

00:05:41.258 --> 00:05:44.091
To embrace cancer and a long dying

00:05:46.358 --> 00:05:49.841
as a blessing in that, it wasn't quick

00:05:49.841 --> 00:05:52.152
and we were able to say
everything we wanted to say.

00:05:52.152 --> 00:05:53.656
To be able to look at any instance

00:05:53.656 --> 00:05:57.384
from a place of education and nourishment

00:05:57.384 --> 00:06:00.599
as opposed to a deficit
is one of the things

00:06:00.599 --> 00:06:04.766
that Oprah taught me and
gave me and was a huge gift.

00:06:05.862 --> 00:06:09.862
If your dream only includes
you, it's too small.

00:06:11.643 --> 00:06:13.721
If that dream is just
about the thing you want

00:06:13.721 --> 00:06:16.337
to accomplish and you don't
even know why you want it,

00:06:16.337 --> 00:06:18.953
I mean it's too small.

00:06:18.953 --> 00:06:22.358
It may take your attention,
but you're not really winning.

00:06:22.358 --> 00:06:24.969
You may achieve it, but
you're not growing from it.

00:06:24.969 --> 00:06:26.384
You're just going from thing to thing.

00:06:26.384 --> 00:06:28.361
It may look like success from the outside,

00:06:28.361 --> 00:06:30.841
but if you don't even truly
know why you're doing it,

00:06:30.841 --> 00:06:32.753
then your cause and effect will be off,

00:06:32.753 --> 00:06:34.978
and you're not going to be
fully, truly living your dream.

00:06:34.978 --> 00:06:38.393
So, I feel a little weird to tell you

00:06:38.393 --> 00:06:40.289
that when you win awards
and a light is on you

00:06:40.289 --> 00:06:41.977
that it's not going to be enough.

00:06:41.977 --> 00:06:43.113
But that's what I'm telling you.

00:06:43.113 --> 00:06:45.745
And I wish that someone would
have told me that early.

00:06:45.745 --> 00:06:47.390
If we limit our visions to those things

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:49.479
outside of us to validate us,

00:06:49.479 --> 00:06:52.903
we're making an intentional
error that might very well

00:06:52.903 --> 00:06:55.094
bring the outside thing you want,

00:06:55.094 --> 00:06:57.302
but it'll ring hollow in the end.

00:06:57.302 --> 00:06:59.528
Why, because none of
that shit really matters.

00:06:59.528 --> 00:07:00.492
Am I like a consensus builder?

00:07:00.492 --> 00:07:01.490
I try to be.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:03.798
I mean, I've been crew,
so I know how it feels

00:07:03.798 --> 00:07:06.291
to be on a set where
there's not, you know,

00:07:06.291 --> 00:07:08.563
warmth emanating from behind the camera.

00:07:08.563 --> 00:07:11.347
When crew is kind of just walked past,

00:07:11.347 --> 00:07:12.755
and no one knows your name,

00:07:12.755 --> 00:07:14.067
and no one really cares what you're doing

00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:15.836
even though you're working damn hard.

00:07:15.836 --> 00:07:18.335
And so that's something
that also, I can't abide.

00:07:18.335 --> 00:07:20.207
I can't have it.

00:07:20.207 --> 00:07:24.040
So for me, I guess what
some of what you hear,

00:07:24.941 --> 00:07:27.181
or how people might feel is just because

00:07:27.181 --> 00:07:29.669
I'm treating them like human beings.

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:30.502
You know what I mean?

00:07:30.502 --> 00:07:31.805
Treating people the way
I wanted to be treated

00:07:31.805 --> 00:07:33.436
when I was working on these pictures.

00:07:33.436 --> 00:07:35.781
Working my ass off on
these pictures, you know?

00:07:35.781 --> 00:07:36.614
- [Interviewer] Mmhmm.

00:07:36.614 --> 00:07:38.665
- And you know, sometimes often feeling

00:07:38.665 --> 00:07:41.161
like I was invisible.

00:07:41.161 --> 00:07:43.006
And so I just try to bear witness

00:07:43.006 --> 00:07:46.573
to everyone who's doing anything
on behalf of these movies.

00:07:46.573 --> 00:07:48.129
- I find it quite inspiring you were

00:07:48.129 --> 00:07:50.504
in film publicity originally.

00:07:50.504 --> 00:07:52.576
And then at a certain point you decided

00:07:52.576 --> 00:07:53.960
that you wanted to direct.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.559
Can you tell us how you
got from there to here?

00:07:58.559 --> 00:08:00.589
- Well I was one of those
ladies standing on the side.

00:08:00.589 --> 00:08:03.845
And they work hard and no one ever really

00:08:03.845 --> 00:08:05.133
gives them a shout out.

00:08:05.133 --> 00:08:08.850
I thank you all, the
publicists in the corner.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.908
And as a person who loved
film and wanted to make film,

00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:13.841
you're standing there
supporting filmmakers

00:08:13.841 --> 00:08:14.873
telling their stories,

00:08:14.873 --> 00:08:16.456
and it's something that I loved doing.

00:08:16.456 --> 00:08:19.673
But for me, there was always
a little bit of, I don't know,

00:08:19.673 --> 00:08:20.951
not jealousy, but just like,

00:08:20.951 --> 00:08:22.964
"Wow that's great what they're doing."

00:08:22.964 --> 00:08:25.396
And so at the moment when
I actually started to write

00:08:25.396 --> 00:08:27.045
and tried to direct my own stuff,

00:08:27.045 --> 00:08:29.299
I was still doing publicity
for other peoples' films.

00:08:29.299 --> 00:08:31.285
I was trying to make my own films.

00:08:31.285 --> 00:08:33.636
I was asking for
permission, pitching people,

00:08:33.636 --> 00:08:35.684
trying to get contacts and network

00:08:35.684 --> 00:08:37.889
and do all the things that don't matter.

00:08:37.889 --> 00:08:39.969
Because the only thing
that matters is your work.

00:08:39.969 --> 00:08:41.817
And so, when I finally realized that

00:08:41.817 --> 00:08:43.385
and started to make small things,

00:08:43.385 --> 00:08:45.209
and try to move them around festivals,

00:08:45.209 --> 00:08:48.297
and just you know, just share my work,

00:08:48.297 --> 00:08:51.009
you know the energy behind
that positive intention

00:08:51.009 --> 00:08:52.841
started the ball rolling.

00:08:52.841 --> 00:08:55.458
And I've only been making
films for five years.

00:08:55.458 --> 00:08:59.588
And I only quit my job
full-time three years ago.

00:08:59.588 --> 00:09:01.613
Because I was still holding onto publicity

00:09:01.613 --> 00:09:03.325
as a security blanket.

00:09:03.325 --> 00:09:07.075
And so, yeah, I look at
each year that's past

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.700
and all I've tried to do
is continue to make work

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:15.028
whether I have the money to
make a film, a narrative, a doc,

00:09:15.028 --> 00:09:19.390
a commercial, a TV show,
directed an episode of Scandal,

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:21.829
a documentary about Venus Williams.

00:09:21.829 --> 00:09:24.675
Whatever story I want to
tell through whatever medium.

00:09:24.675 --> 00:09:28.117
Short medium, TV medium,
commercial medium,

00:09:28.117 --> 00:09:30.373
full length, documentary, feature.

00:09:30.373 --> 00:09:33.849
That's something that I
really learned from Spike.

00:09:33.849 --> 00:09:34.771
Lee, not Jones.

00:09:34.771 --> 00:09:36.004
I'm sure Jones is nice.

00:09:36.004 --> 00:09:38.333
I was talking to a lady,
she was like Spike Jones.

00:09:38.333 --> 00:09:39.916
No lady, Spike Lee.

00:09:41.629 --> 00:09:44.670
But you know, remember he
did commercials with Jordan.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.685
He's done almost as many
docs as he's done narratives.

00:09:47.685 --> 00:09:50.277
He's made a film every
year for the past 20 years.

00:09:50.277 --> 00:09:53.118
He's really the blueprint of just not even

00:09:53.118 --> 00:09:54.366
a black filmmaker, a filmmaker who has

00:09:54.366 --> 00:09:56.429
just continued to make work.

00:09:56.429 --> 00:09:58.681
And so that's how we're here now.

00:09:58.681 --> 00:09:59.873
Favorite thing to say my mom

00:09:59.873 --> 00:10:01.553
is treat others like
you'd like to be treated.

00:10:01.553 --> 00:10:02.861
She always, always says that.

00:10:02.861 --> 00:10:04.338
Since I was a little girl.

00:10:04.338 --> 00:10:05.946
So that was just something that,

00:10:05.946 --> 00:10:07.922
you know I carry onto my sets with me,

00:10:07.922 --> 00:10:11.306
carry into my corporate life,
certainly my personal life.

00:10:11.306 --> 00:10:13.562
You know, my love life, in my life.

00:10:13.562 --> 00:10:17.565
Always trying to put the
person that I'm relating to

00:10:17.565 --> 00:10:20.485
ahead of myself, in a way
that doesn't diminish me.

00:10:20.485 --> 00:10:23.402
In a way that actually enlarges me.

00:10:24.349 --> 00:10:26.422
And so that's a fine balance.

00:10:26.422 --> 00:10:27.981
It's something that you have to learn.

00:10:27.981 --> 00:10:29.901
Because you can be taken advantage of.

00:10:29.901 --> 00:10:32.442
But there's a way to do
it where you're actually

00:10:32.442 --> 00:10:34.325
getting more than you're giving

00:10:34.325 --> 00:10:38.127
when you allow another
person to kind of be first.

00:10:38.127 --> 00:10:40.910
And so, that's a direct
lesson from my mom.

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:42.390
- [Interviewer] Do you have
time to even think about

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:45.138
like where you are in the
award season at this point?

00:10:45.138 --> 00:10:47.083
- No, I don't have a dress
for the Golden Globes.

00:10:47.083 --> 00:10:48.403
And it's two days before Globes.

00:10:48.403 --> 00:10:49.508
- [Interviewer] It's two days from now!

00:10:49.508 --> 00:10:50.505
- I don't, I don't.

00:10:50.505 --> 00:10:51.752
- [Interviewer] Who's
going to come to your house

00:10:51.752 --> 00:10:52.601
and give you a dress?

00:10:52.601 --> 00:10:53.434
Somebody has to be doing that.

00:10:53.434 --> 00:10:54.537
- It'll work out, it'll work out.

00:10:54.537 --> 00:10:56.630
It always works out. It always works out.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:11:00.205
But no, I mean, I think it's only because

00:11:00.205 --> 00:11:03.447
I was a publicist before,
and for whatever thoughts

00:11:03.447 --> 00:11:06.494
I used to have about how much
that was a waste of time.

00:11:06.494 --> 00:11:08.958
I should have been making
films, I started too late.

00:11:08.958 --> 00:11:12.773
You know, I should have gone
to NYU or Howard Film School

00:11:12.773 --> 00:11:15.641
and learned all this, and
I should have realized

00:11:15.641 --> 00:11:16.833
what I wanted to do earlier.

00:11:16.833 --> 00:11:18.903
I started making films
in my early, mid-30s.

00:11:18.903 --> 00:11:20.599
I was a publicist, you know.

00:11:20.599 --> 00:11:22.351
I mean the odds that that
was ever going to work,

00:11:22.351 --> 00:11:24.303
and I'd ever be sitting here with you.

00:11:24.303 --> 00:11:26.452
That I'd be here on opening day.

00:11:26.452 --> 00:11:27.642
Today's opening day.

00:11:27.642 --> 00:11:28.563
- [Interviewer] Amazing.

00:11:28.563 --> 00:11:30.739
- The last film that I had in theaters,

00:11:30.739 --> 00:11:32.738
I distributed myself on 22 screens.

00:11:32.738 --> 00:11:35.635
Now it's on 2,300 screens
with Paramount Pictures,

00:11:35.635 --> 00:11:36.724
who used to be my client.

00:11:36.724 --> 00:11:39.569
Like that, that stuff only
just puts me in a place

00:11:39.569 --> 00:11:42.416
of just being grateful that
I got to make the film.

00:11:42.416 --> 00:11:44.687
And that I continue to
be able to make films.

00:11:44.687 --> 00:11:46.620
So the dresses and the numbers,

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:50.287
all that stuff is just
less important to me.

00:11:52.129 --> 00:11:53.386
- [Ava DuVernay] It's
interesting to talk to

00:11:53.386 --> 00:11:55.169
creative people about
the way their minds work.

00:11:55.169 --> 00:11:57.394
Sometimes it sounds kind of freaky.

00:11:57.394 --> 00:11:59.209
When you're a creative person,

00:11:59.209 --> 00:12:00.889
and you're nurturing that within yourself,

00:12:00.889 --> 00:12:03.521
you open yourself up to a lot of discovery

00:12:03.521 --> 00:12:05.273
that comes from unexpected places.

00:12:05.273 --> 00:12:06.497
Which is really wonderful.

00:12:06.497 --> 00:12:08.997
(piano music)

00:12:09.896 --> 00:12:12.185
I mean for me, the writing
process is very much about

00:12:12.185 --> 00:12:14.434
when I have a really, really
good idea of what I'm writing.

00:12:14.434 --> 00:12:16.817
I need to have notes, a good outline,

00:12:16.817 --> 00:12:18.847
thought it through, have some dialogue.

00:12:18.847 --> 00:12:20.911
Like, really be clear
about what I'm doing.

00:12:20.911 --> 00:12:23.135
And all that comes from
being out in the world.

00:12:23.135 --> 00:12:25.295
My films are low budget,
independent films.

00:12:25.295 --> 00:12:27.719
Very specifically focused
around communities of color,

00:12:27.719 --> 00:12:29.503
and people of color,
and families of color,

00:12:29.503 --> 00:12:32.508
and finding the universality
in the specificity

00:12:32.508 --> 00:12:33.771
of those stories.

00:12:33.771 --> 00:12:35.252
When you're making a low
budget film, a film for

00:12:35.252 --> 00:12:38.164
$50,000 or $200,000,
there's nowhere to hide.

00:12:38.164 --> 00:12:39.532
It's just people.

00:12:39.532 --> 00:12:41.647
Usually people talking, or not talking.

00:12:41.647 --> 00:12:42.761
But you're on these spaces.

00:12:42.761 --> 00:12:44.681
You're on behavior.

00:12:44.681 --> 00:12:47.159
And so you really have to
be able to understand that.

00:12:47.159 --> 00:12:49.304
And so that comes from living.

00:12:49.304 --> 00:12:51.176
Being a black woman walking around.

00:12:51.176 --> 00:12:52.791
It comes from observing people.

00:12:52.791 --> 00:12:54.672
It comes from asking questions.

00:12:54.672 --> 00:12:57.056
And so I find that, you
know, I've got to get out.

00:12:57.056 --> 00:12:58.872
You know, and you got to be around people.

00:12:58.872 --> 00:13:00.623
And in Los Angeles it's such a sprawl.

00:13:00.623 --> 00:13:02.348
It's such a huge place.

00:13:02.348 --> 00:13:04.741
And downtown, you could actually
rub shoulders with people.

00:13:04.741 --> 00:13:09.088
You can actually see faces that
are not behind a car window.

00:13:09.088 --> 00:13:10.824
You know what I mean?

00:13:10.824 --> 00:13:12.650
The best thing is when you see
people and you're wondering,

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:13.483
"What's their story?

00:13:13.483 --> 00:13:14.316
"How do they fit together?

00:13:14.316 --> 00:13:15.149
"Where did they come from?

00:13:15.149 --> 00:13:16.242
"Where are they going?

00:13:16.242 --> 00:13:17.238
"What's their connection?

00:13:17.238 --> 00:13:20.006
Sometimes I'll even ask,
and people want to talk.

00:13:20.006 --> 00:13:22.694
You know, and it may not be anything

00:13:22.694 --> 00:13:24.654
that's directly related to the work

00:13:24.654 --> 00:13:25.833
that you're writing that day,

00:13:25.833 --> 00:13:28.583
but that energy from people,
that stuff is kind of like,

00:13:28.583 --> 00:13:30.862
you know, seeds on fertile ground

00:13:30.862 --> 00:13:32.966
that helps with the writing.

00:13:32.966 --> 00:13:34.990
For example, one of these
great places to kind of just

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:37.966
find a good corner is a spot
called The Last Bookstore.

00:13:37.966 --> 00:13:39.539
It's such a great space.

00:13:39.539 --> 00:13:41.115
That density that I talked about,

00:13:41.115 --> 00:13:43.596
it's dense with books and people

00:13:43.596 --> 00:13:46.068
and it's not too busy,
but it's just enough.

00:13:46.068 --> 00:13:48.996
The right flow of the odd
character that comes in and out.

00:13:48.996 --> 00:13:52.088
You know, it's part of what I prefer

00:13:52.088 --> 00:13:54.061
to have around me when I'm writing.

00:13:54.061 --> 00:13:55.765
I'm outlining, I'm playing with dialogue,

00:13:55.765 --> 00:13:58.292
I'm figuring out what
the beginning, middle,

00:13:58.292 --> 00:13:59.841
and end of the scene is.

00:13:59.841 --> 00:14:01.252
Is it moving the film forward?

00:14:01.252 --> 00:14:05.391
Really trying to pin
down those big questions.

00:14:05.391 --> 00:14:07.451
I consider movie watching people watching,

00:14:07.451 --> 00:14:08.791
because you're watching people.

00:14:08.791 --> 00:14:09.624
You're watching behavior,

00:14:09.624 --> 00:14:11.915
actors kind of mimic human behavior.

00:14:11.915 --> 00:14:13.490
We're here at the Downtown Independent,

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.362
which is one of my favorite
places to be inspired.

00:14:16.362 --> 00:14:19.546
It's a great one-house movie
theater in downtown L.A.

00:14:19.546 --> 00:14:20.642
I really love it here.

00:14:20.642 --> 00:14:23.154
I love coming down and
watching other peoples' films.

00:14:23.154 --> 00:14:24.907
I love coming in here and
watching my own films.

00:14:24.907 --> 00:14:27.828
Wherever I find myself
these spaces that inspire,

00:14:27.828 --> 00:14:31.566
it leads to the sparking
of my imagination.

00:14:31.566 --> 00:14:35.419
I definitely am recognizing
when I'm being hit and inspired

00:14:35.419 --> 00:14:38.095
and then making sure
that I'm logging that,

00:14:38.095 --> 00:14:40.286
or documenting that in some way.

00:14:40.286 --> 00:14:42.793
I take pictures a lot, and then
yeah, I take a lot of notes,

00:14:42.793 --> 00:14:45.379
and I find that I used to just
scribble on scraps of paper,

00:14:45.379 --> 00:14:47.842
but now more and more I rely on the phone.

00:14:47.842 --> 00:14:50.118
I'll put in the subject header, "XO".

00:14:50.118 --> 00:14:52.006
I don't know, that's my code.

00:14:52.006 --> 00:14:54.367
Hugs and kisses to myself of inspiration.

00:14:54.367 --> 00:14:55.654
It's so corny.

00:14:55.654 --> 00:14:57.630
And so, whenever I'm ready to sit down

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.878
or I need to pull in
something that I've seen,

00:14:59.878 --> 00:15:02.335
I just search XO and everything comes up.

00:15:02.335 --> 00:15:04.001
Whether it's a picture or a thought.

00:15:04.001 --> 00:15:06.484
A lot of my director's work is happening

00:15:06.484 --> 00:15:08.492
during the screenwriting process.

00:15:08.492 --> 00:15:09.868
Questions about character,

00:15:09.868 --> 00:15:12.884
and being able to answer
those questions for actors.

00:15:12.884 --> 00:15:15.036
A lot of that stuff I'm
answering for myself

00:15:15.036 --> 00:15:17.964
as I'm collecting my material to write.

00:15:17.964 --> 00:15:21.276
I have a special exercise that I hold

00:15:21.276 --> 00:15:22.907
very close to my heart,

00:15:22.907 --> 00:15:25.490
for when I am in a rough patch.

00:15:26.927 --> 00:15:28.743
It's called procrastination.

00:15:28.743 --> 00:15:33.318
I will put it off until
something else strikes me.

00:15:33.318 --> 00:15:35.888
I wish I had a magic talisman

00:15:35.888 --> 00:15:38.518
to pull me back into the space.

00:15:38.518 --> 00:15:39.398
I just don't.

00:15:39.398 --> 00:15:41.159
I've got to be in the right head space

00:15:41.159 --> 00:15:42.518
or else you're just forcing it

00:15:42.518 --> 00:15:44.180
and it just never comes out right.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:47.071
I know there were times when, you know,

00:15:47.071 --> 00:15:48.973
I wasn't working in a creative way,

00:15:48.973 --> 00:15:53.635
that I felt very unhappy that
I wasn't living my best life.

00:15:53.635 --> 00:15:57.299
And so for me, to be creating,
to be around creative people,

00:15:57.299 --> 00:15:58.878
is something that's really necessary

00:15:58.878 --> 00:16:00.788
for my own personal happiness.

00:16:00.788 --> 00:16:02.257
And I'm just really fortunate

00:16:02.257 --> 00:16:03.765
that I get the opportunity to do it,

00:16:03.765 --> 00:16:05.995
because everyone that needs that,

00:16:05.995 --> 00:16:08.146
doesn't get the opportunity to do it.

00:16:08.146 --> 00:16:09.771
And I was one of those
people for a long time.

00:16:09.771 --> 00:16:11.563
So I'm happy to be in this place.

00:16:11.563 --> 00:16:12.671
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:16:12.671 --> 00:16:15.456
I made this video because
Ella Franklin asked me to.

00:16:15.456 --> 00:16:17.012
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:16:17.012 --> 00:16:18.707
that you want me to profile next,

00:16:18.707 --> 00:16:20.676
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:20.676 --> 00:16:22.812
I'd also love to know
which of the top 10 rules

00:16:22.812 --> 00:16:25.027
you heard today had the
biggest impact on you.

00:16:25.027 --> 00:16:26.241
Leave it in the comments below.

00:16:26.241 --> 00:16:27.634
I'll join the discussion.

00:16:27.634 --> 00:16:29.137
Thank you so much for watching.

00:16:29.137 --> 00:16:31.298
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:16:31.298 --> 00:16:33.715
(jet engine)

